# Target C — Section 122: exact regressions for uncharged host proliferation and generic detour upper bounds

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only regression / route audit.

Section 121 permits a macroscopic defect-face cycle to be split into two near-geodesic
arcs at `o(D)` route excess. That operation adds an antipodal pair of unit host
directions to the signed-return problem. It is tempting to ignore that host
proliferation and keep a positive per-rhombus return constant.

That is false, even for an exact finite triangle-free unit-separated rhombic complex.
The same model also shows that the Section-116 two-arc detour cannot have a generic
`o(D)` upper bound derived only from convexity, unit separation, and rhombic structure.
Any such upper bound must use source-specific global-minimality information.

---

## 122.1 A strict oblique unit lattice patch

Fix

\[
{\pi\over3}<\varphi\le {\pi\over2}
\]

and unit vectors

\[
u=(1,0),
\qquad
v=(\cos\varphi,\sin\varphi).
\]

For positive integers `m,n`, define

\[
X_{m,n}:=
\{iu+jv:0\le i\le m,\ 0\le j\le n\}.
\tag{122.1}
\]

### Lemma 122.1 — exact separation and contact graph

`X_{m,n}` is unit-separated. Equality `|x-y|=1` occurs exactly when the difference is
one of

\[
\pm u,
\qquad
\pm v.
\tag{122.2}
\]

Hence its unit-contact graph is the rectangular grid graph and is triangle-free.

### Proof

Every difference of two points has the form

\[
au+bv,
\qquad a,b\in\mathbb Z,
\]

with `(a,b) ne (0,0)`. Put `c=cos(varphi)`, so

\[
0\le c<{1\over2}.
\]

Then

\[
|au+bv|^2=a^2+b^2+2ab c.
\tag{122.3}
\]

If `ab>=0`, the right side is at least `a^2+b^2>=1`. Equality requires exactly one
of `|a|,|b|` to equal one and the other to vanish.

If `ab<0`, put `A=|a|`, `B=|b|`. Then

\[
|au+bv|^2
=A^2+B^2-2ABc
=(A-B)^2+2AB(1-c).
\tag{122.4}
\]

If `A ne B`, the first term is at least one and the second is positive. If `A=B`,
then

\[
|au+bv|^2=2A^2(1-c)>A^2\ge1.
\]

Thus the only unit differences are (122.2). The resulting grid graph is bipartite, so
it contains no triangle. `square`

Every bounded grid cell is therefore a strict unit rhombus of angle `varphi>pi/3`.
There are exactly `mn` rhombi and total rhombic area

\[
\boxed{A_4=mn\sin\varphi>0.}
\tag{122.5}
\]

---

## 122.2 Four physical route hosts annihilate signed return completely

Orient the four side walks of the boundary parallelogram consecutively. They are
straight unit-edge routes with hosts

\[
Q=\{u,v,-u,-v\}.
\tag{122.6}
\]

Each side walk has route excess zero, so the total route excess is

\[
\boxed{E=0.}
\tag{122.7}
\]

Every complete train of the rhombic grid has its two physical terminal edge
occurrences on the corresponding pair of opposite sides. Assign each endpoint to its
actual side occurrence. The assignment is occurrence-injective and orientation
matching.

For a `u`-direction train,

\[
w_Q(u)
=\max_{q\in Q}q\cdot u-
 \min_{q\in Q}q\cdot u
=1-(-1)=2,
\]

so

\[
2-w_Q(u)=0.
\tag{122.8}
\]

Likewise

\[
2-w_Q(v)=0.
\tag{122.9}
\]

These are the only two train directions. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\sum_\tau(2-w_Q(u_\tau))=0
}
\tag{122.10}
\]

while `A_4>0` by (122.5).

### Corollary 122.2 — no host-count-free positive area-return theorem

There is no universal constant `c>0` such that every triangle-free strict-rhombic
complex with occurrence-injectively routed train endpoints satisfies

\[
E\ge {c\over d}A_4
\]

when the route host set may contain four unconstrained unit directions.

In particular, after Section 121 splits macroscopic defect corridors, one may not keep
Section 116's positive three-host constant while ignoring the new corridor hosts.
Host diversity must itself be bounded or charged to a physical source currency.

This regression is exact; it is not a limiting construction.

---

## 122.3 The same model has a linear two-arc detour

The convex hull of `X_{m,n}` is the parallelogram with side lengths `m,n`. Its outer
unit-edge walk has length

\[
L=2(m+n),
\qquad
P_{\rm exc}=0.
\tag{122.11}
\]

Because `0<=cos(varphi)<1/2`, the longer diagonal is

\[
d=|mu+nv|
=\sqrt{m^2+n^2+2mn\cos\varphi}.
\tag{122.12}
\]

The Section-116 two-arc detour is therefore

\[
\boxed{
L+P_{\rm exc}-2d
=2\left(
 m+n-\sqrt{m^2+n^2+2mn\cos\varphi}
\right)>0.
}
\tag{122.13}
\]

For the square-indexed sequence `m=n=N`,

\[
d=2N\cos(\varphi/2)
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
L-2d
=4N\bigl(1-\cos(\varphi/2)\bigr)
=\left({2\over\cos(\varphi/2)}-2\right)d.
}
\tag{122.14}
\]

The coefficient in parentheses is a fixed positive constant for every fixed
`varphi>pi/3`. Thus the two-arc detour is genuinely `Theta(d)`.

### Corollary 122.3 — generic convex geometry cannot give `o(D)` detour

No theorem using only

- unit separation,
- triangle-freeness,
- convex containment,
- a strict-rhombic contact complex, and
- a diameter-scale container

can force

\[
L+P_{\rm exc}-2d=o(d).
\]

Any upper bound of the form required to contradict Section 116 or Section 120 must use
an additional hypothesis absent from the oblique patch. In Target C the natural
missing hypotheses are global diameter minimality, the loaded-diameter/KKT structure,
near-disk full-core saturation, or the globally restricted interface ledgers.

---

## 122.4 Relation to the Type-II route

The oblique patch is **not** asserted to be a global diameter minimizer and is not a
Type-II survivor. Therefore it does not disprove C-II and does not contradict Sections
116, 120, or 121.

Its role is narrower and binding:

1. **Route B:** splitting a face into near-geodesic arcs trades route excess for host
   directions. Four appropriately placed physical hosts can make the signed-return
   deficit zero across positive rhombic area. A valid all-regime theorem must charge
   the number, span, or directional coverage of the new hosts.
2. **Route A:** a linear outer detour is compatible with an exact triangle-free
   rhombic packing. A conflicting upper bound cannot come from generic convexity or
   train geometry alone.
3. **Exactness:** the obstruction survives at finite `m,n`; there is no asymptotic
   error to absorb.

The next useful theorem should therefore be source-specific. A strong form would show
that, in a Type-II global minimizer, every additional antipodal host pair capable of
shadowing a macroscopic train phase consumes a quantitatively disjoint amount of
inactive-interface or diameter-load currency. Summing that charge against the
Section-120 aggregate area floor could then yield a contradiction.

---

## 122.5 Scope audit

- The separation proof treats every integer difference, not only nearest grid
  neighbors.
- Triangle-freeness follows from the exact contact graph, not from a visual lattice
  heuristic.
- Physical boundary occurrences are used once each in the route assignment.
- The four hosts are the actual chord directions of straight boundary routes, not
  artificial support vectors.
- No source point is deleted.
- The construction is a regression for overstrong intermediate lemmas only; it is not
  claimed to satisfy global minimality.

**Disposition:** rigorous regression. C-II remains open.
